The driver supports the Geode CS5530A and SC1100 controllers, and
provides  the  interface  with  the hardware for the driver.  The
0x0002 flag forces the driver to disable on  chipsets  for  which
would  normally be enabled.  This can be used as a debugging aid,
or to work around problems where the controller is  wired  up  to
the  system  incorrectly.   The SC1100 controller requires 4‐byte
aligned data transfers and cannot handle transfers of exactly  64
kilobytes.   The  CS5530 multifunction chip/core’s section claims
to be capable of mode 2 but in practice using  that  mode  swamps
the  controller so badly that limits the negotiation to mode 1 so
that the other functions of this chip continue to work.  The  en‐
gine  in  the  CS5530  can only do transfers on cache‐line bound‐
aries.  Attempts to perform on any other alignment will crash the
system.  This problem may also exist  in  the  SC1100  since  the
CS5530  was  its direct predecessor, and it is not clear that Na‐
tional Semiconductor fixed any bugs in it.  The driver  will  re‐
ject attempts to to buffers not aligned to the required boundary.
The  disk driver will back off to mode to accomplish these trans‐
fer requests, at reduced system performance.
